### What are Schema Registry compatibility modes?

The Schema Registry tracks schemas per subject, which is usually `topicName-value`. Each time you register a new schema version, the registry compares it against existing versions using the subject's compatibility mode and rejects the registration if the rule is violated. This check happens at registration time, which is far better than discovering the break at runtime in production.

```java
package com.bank.schema;

// Setting compatibility mode via REST API
// PUT /config/transfers-value
// {"compatibility": "BACKWARD"}

public class CompatibilityConfig {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Compatibility modes:");
        System.out.println("  BACKWARD            -- new reader, old data");
        System.out.println("  FORWARD             -- old reader, new data");
        System.out.println("  FULL                -- both directions");
        System.out.println("  NONE                -- no checks");
        System.out.println("  BACKWARD_TRANSITIVE -- against ALL versions");
        System.out.println("  FORWARD_TRANSITIVE  -- against ALL versions");
        System.out.println("  FULL_TRANSITIVE     -- against ALL versions");
    }
}
```

```text
Compatibility modes:
  BACKWARD            -- new reader, old data
  FORWARD             -- old reader, new data
  FULL                -- both directions
  NONE                -- no checks
  BACKWARD_TRANSITIVE -- against ALL versions
  FORWARD_TRANSITIVE  -- against ALL versions
  FULL_TRANSITIVE     -- against ALL versions
```

### Why do compatibility modes exist?

In a banking system with multiple teams, producers and consumers are deployed independently. The fraud team might release a consumer on Tuesday while the transfers team releases the producer on Thursday. Compatibility modes guarantee that these independent deployments do not break each other. Without them, a schema change by the producer could silently corrupt data for every consumer.

```java
package com.bank.deploy;

public class IndependentDeployment {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("BACKWARD deployment order:");
        System.out.println("  1. Deploy consumer with new schema (reads old + new)");
        System.out.println("  2. Deploy producer with new schema");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("FORWARD deployment order:");
        System.out.println("  1. Deploy producer with new schema");
        System.out.println("  2. Deploy consumer with new schema (old reads new)");
    }
}
```

```text
BACKWARD deployment order:
  1. Deploy consumer with new schema (reads old + new)
  2. Deploy producer with new schema

FORWARD deployment order:
  1. Deploy producer with new schema
  2. Deploy consumer with new schema (old reads new)
```

### How does BACKWARD compatibility work?

BACKWARD is the default and most common mode in event systems. A consumer upgraded to the new schema can still read messages produced with the old schema. Safe backward changes are deleting a field and adding a field that has a default.

```json
// Version 1 of Transfer schema
{
  "type": "record",
  "name": "Transfer",
  "namespace": "com.bank.avro",
  "fields": [
    {"name": "accountId", "type": "string"},
    {"name": "amount",    "type": "string"},
    {"name": "type",      "type": "string"}
  ]
}

// Version 2 -- adds currency with a default (BACKWARD compatible)
{
  "type": "record",
  "name": "Transfer",
  "namespace": "com.bank.avro",
  "fields": [
    {"name": "accountId", "type": "string"},
    {"name": "amount",    "type": "string"},
    {"name": "type",      "type": "string"},
    {"name": "currency",  "type": "string", "default": "INR"}
  ]
}
```

### Why does adding a required field break compatibility?

In Avro, a field without a default is required. If you add a required field, a consumer reading an old message that was written before the field existed finds no value for it and no default to fall back on, so decoding fails. The registry rejects this registration under BACKWARD mode.

```java
package com.bank.schema;

public class RequiredFieldBreak {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Scenario: adding 'currency' without a default");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Old record written with v1:");
        System.out.println("  {accountId: SAV2001, amount: 50000, type: DEBIT}");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("New consumer reads with v2 schema:");
        System.out.println("  Expects: currency field");
        System.out.println("  Found  : no currency field, no default");
        System.out.println("  Result : DECODE FAILS");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Fix: add default to currency field");
        System.out.println("  {name: currency, type: string, default: INR}");
        System.out.println("  Old record decoded: currency = INR (from default)");
    }
}
```

```text
Scenario: adding 'currency' without a default

Old record written with v1:
  {accountId: SAV2001, amount: 50000, type: DEBIT}

New consumer reads with v2 schema:
  Expects: currency field
  Found  : no currency field, no default
  Result : DECODE FAILS

Fix: add default to currency field
  {name: currency, type: string, default: INR}
  Old record decoded: currency = INR (from default)
```

### How do you validate compatibility in CI?

The `schema-registry-maven-plugin` can test compatibility during the build, so a bad schema fails CI instead of failing a consumer in production.

```xml
<!-- pom.xml -- schema-registry-maven-plugin -->
<plugin>
    <groupId>io.confluent</groupId>
    <artifactId>kafka-schema-registry-maven-plugin</artifactId>
    <version>7.5.0</version>
    <configuration>
        <schemaRegistryUrls>
            <param>http://schema-registry:8081</param>
        </schemaRegistryUrls>
        <subjects>
            <transfers-value>
                src/main/avro/Transfer.avsc
            </transfers-value>
        </subjects>
    </configuration>
    <executions>
        <execution>
            <goals>
                <goal>test-compatibility</goal>
            </goals>
        </execution>
    </executions>
</plugin>
```

```bash
# Run compatibility check in CI
mvn schema-registry:test-compatibility
```

### What are the rules for each mode? (comparison)

| Change | BACKWARD | FORWARD | FULL | NONE |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Add field with default | Safe | Safe | Safe | Safe |
| Add field without default | Breaks | Safe | Breaks | Safe |
| Delete field with default | Safe | Safe | Safe | Safe |
| Delete field without default | Safe | Breaks | Breaks | Safe |
| Rename field | Breaks | Breaks | Breaks | Safe |

```java
package com.bank.schema;

public class CompatibilityRules {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Safe changes per mode:");
        System.out.println("  BACKWARD : add with default, delete any");
        System.out.println("  FORWARD  : add any, delete with default");
        System.out.println("  FULL     : add with default, delete with default");
        System.out.println("  NONE     : anything goes (dangerous!)");
    }
}
```

```text
Safe changes per mode:
  BACKWARD : add with default, delete any
  FORWARD  : add any, delete with default
  FULL     : add with default, delete with default
  NONE     : anything goes (dangerous!)
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
package com.bank.mistake;

public class SchemaMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: adding required field to an existing topic
        System.out.println("WRONG: {name: currency, type: string}");
        System.out.println("  Registry rejects under BACKWARD mode");
        System.out.println();

        // CORRECT: add field with a default
        System.out.println("CORRECT: {name: currency, type: string, default: INR}");
        System.out.println("  Registry accepts, old records decode with INR");
        System.out.println();

        // WRONG: setting NONE in production
        System.out.println("WRONG: compatibility=NONE on transfers-value");
        System.out.println("  Allows breaking changes without any check");
        System.out.println();

        // CORRECT: use BACKWARD or FULL
        System.out.println("CORRECT: compatibility=BACKWARD on transfers-value");
        System.out.println("  Registry enforces safe evolution");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: {name: currency, type: string}
  Registry rejects under BACKWARD mode

CORRECT: {name: currency, type: string, default: INR}
  Registry accepts, old records decode with INR

WRONG: compatibility=NONE on transfers-value
  Allows breaking changes without any check

CORRECT: compatibility=BACKWARD on transfers-value
  Registry enforces safe evolution
```

### When should you use each mode?

- Use **BACKWARD** (the default) for most event topics where consumers are upgraded before producers.
- Use **FORWARD** when producers must be upgraded first and consumers catch up later.
- Use **FULL** for shared schemas across many teams where maximum safety is required.
- Use **NONE** only during early development on brand-new topics, and switch to BACKWARD before going live.
- Use **transitive variants** when consumers may read data many versions old, such as historical replay from the beginning of a topic.

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer understands that compatibility modes turn schema evolution from a coordinated, risky, big-bang release into a safe, independent, incremental process. The registry check runs at registration time, ideally in CI through the schema-registry-maven-plugin, so a bad schema fails the build instead of failing a consumer in production. The deployment order depends on the mode: BACKWARD means consumers first, FORWARD means producers first. Transitive modes matter when consumers replay from the start of a topic and must read schemas that are several versions old.
